<appendix id="appendix-cypher">
 <title>Cypher Syntax</title>

 <refentry id="cypher-path">
  <indexterm zone="cypher-path">
   <primary>Cypher Path</primary>
  </indexterm>
  <indexterm zone="cypher-path">
   <primary>pattern</primary>
  </indexterm>

  <refmeta>
   <refentrytitle>Cypher Path</refentrytitle>
   <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
   <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
   <refname>Cypher Path</refname>
   <refpurpose>the pattern and expression syntax every Cypher clause is built from</refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>

  <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
<replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable>:

    [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> [ <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> ] ...

<replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable>:

    ( [ <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] [ : <replaceable class="parameter">label</replaceable> [ ONLY ] ] [ { <replaceable class="parameter">property_map</replaceable> } ] )

<replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable>, one of:

    -[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-&gt;      left to right
    &lt;-[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-      right to left
    -[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-       either direction

<replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable>:

    [ <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] [ : <replaceable class="parameter">label</replaceable> [ ONLY ] ] [ * [ <replaceable class="parameter">min</replaceable> ] [ .. [ <replaceable class="parameter">max</replaceable> ] ] ] [ { <replaceable class="parameter">property_map</replaceable> } ]

shortest path forms, each one a whole <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable>:

    [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] shortestpath( <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> )
    [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] allshortestpaths( <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> )
    <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = dijkstra( <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable>, <replaceable class="parameter">weight</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">qualifier</replaceable> ] [, LIMIT <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable> ] )
    ( <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable>, <replaceable class="parameter">weight_variable</replaceable> ) = dijkstra( <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable>, <replaceable class="parameter">weight</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">qualifier</replaceable> ] [, LIMIT <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable> ] )
</synopsis>
  </refsynopsisdiv>

  <refsect1 id="cypher-path-description">
   <title>Description</title>

   <para>
    A pattern describes a shape in the graph. <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> looks for it,
    <xref linkend="cypher-create"/>, <xref linkend="cypher-merge"/> and
    <xref linkend="cypher-insert"/> build it. The syntax is the same in all four, so this page
    is the one definition of it.
   </para>

   <para>
    A pattern is a chain that starts with a node, and continues with an edge and another node
    as often as needed. Each element may carry a variable, a label and a property map, and
    each of the three is optional. An element with no variable is matched but cannot be
    referred to again. A whole pattern may be given a name with
    <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> and <literal>=</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    A variable bound by a node pattern holds a value of type <type>vertex</type>, one bound by
    an edge pattern a value of type <type>edge</type>, and one bound to a whole pattern a value
    of type <type>graphpath</type>. A variable name is an identifier, so it folds to lower case
    unless it is double quoted, and it may not begin with a digit. A name already in scope is
    not rebound: reusing it constrains the new element to the value the name already holds.
   </para>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-path-nodes">
    <title>Node Patterns</title>

    <para>
     A node pattern is written in parentheses. Empty parentheses match every vertex in the
     graph. A colon and a name restrict it to a label, an identifier in front of the colon
     names it, and a map in braces constrains its properties.
    </para>

<synopsis>
( )
(:person)
(v)
(v:person)
(v:person {name: 'Jack'})
({name: 'Jack'})
(v:person ONLY {name: 'Jack'})
</synopsis>

    <para>
     A property map is an equality test on each key it lists, so
     <literal>(v:person {name: 'Jack', age: 24})</literal> requires both properties to have
     those values. The comparison is a <type>jsonb</type> comparison, which matters when the
     literal's type differs from the stored one. The map may also be a query parameter holding
     a map, which is how an application passes values it did not write into the statement.
    </para>

    <para>
     A label matches its own rows and the rows of every label that inherits from it, because a
     label is a table. <literal>ONLY</literal> narrows the match to the one label. It goes
     after the label and before the property map, and it needs a label:
     <literal>(n ONLY)</literal> raises
     <literal>ONLY must have one label preceding it</literal>.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-path-edges">
    <title>Edge Patterns</title>

    <para>
     An edge pattern is a pair of dashes with square brackets between them. The brackets are
     part of the syntax and are not optional. An arrowhead on one side gives the edge a
     direction, and no arrowhead means either direction. Arrowheads on both sides,
     <literal>&lt;-[ ]-&gt;</literal>, mean the same as none.
    </para>

<synopsis>
-[]-&gt;
&lt;-[]-
-[]-
-[:knows]-&gt;
-[e]-&gt;
-[e:knows]-&gt;
-[e:knows {since: 2015}]-&gt;
-[e:knows ONLY]-&gt;
-[e:knows*1..3]-&gt;
</synopsis>

    <para>
     An edge pattern carries at most one label. Two names separated by <literal>|</literal> are
     refused with <literal>multiple types for relationship not supported</literal>, on a fixed
     length edge, on a variable length edge and inside <literal>shortestpath</literal> alike.
     To match a set of edge labels, leave the edge unlabelled and test
     <literal>label(e)</literal> in <xref linkend="cypher-where"/>.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-path-varlen">
    <title>Variable Length Edge Patterns</title>

    <para>
     An asterisk after the label turns one edge into a run of edges. The bounds follow the
     asterisk, and both are optional.
    </para>

    <informaltable>
     <tgroup cols="2">
      <thead>
       <row>
        <entry>Written</entry>
        <entry>Means</entry>
       </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*2]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>exactly two edges</entry></row>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*1..3]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>one, two or three edges</entry></row>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*2..]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>two or more</entry></row>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*..3]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>up to three, and at least one</entry></row>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>one or more, no upper bound</entry></row>
       <row><entry><literal>-[:related_to*0..1]-&gt;</literal></entry><entry>the vertex itself, or one edge away</entry></row>
      </tbody>
     </tgroup>
    </informaltable>

    <para>
     A lower bound of <literal>0</literal> includes the zero length path, which binds both ends
     of the pattern to the same vertex. A lower bound above the upper bound is rejected when
     the statement is parsed, with <literal>invalid range</literal>.
    </para>

    <para>
     An unbounded traversal terminates even on a cyclic graph, because no path may use the same
     edge twice. A vertex may repeat, so a run may return to where it started, but each run is
     a walk over distinct edges and there are finitely many of those. That is a guarantee about
     termination and not about cost: an unbounded search over a dense graph can still enumerate
     an enormous number of paths. Give the range an upper bound whenever you have any idea what
     it should be.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-path-named">
    <title>Named Paths</title>

    <para>
     <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> and <literal>=</literal> in
     front of a pattern bind the whole path, with its vertices and edges in order.
     <literal>length()</literal> returns the edge count, <literal>nodes()</literal> the
     vertices and <literal>relationships()</literal> the edges.
     <literal>edges()</literal> is a synonym for <literal>relationships()</literal>.
    </para>

    <para>
     This position takes a narrower set of names than an element variable does. An unreserved
     keyword such as <literal>path</literal> is an acceptable element variable but is rejected
     as a path variable unless it is double quoted, so <literal>MATCH path = ...</literal> is a
     syntax error while <literal>MATCH (path) ...</literal> is not. A name that is
     <literal>_agens_default_</literal> or starts with it is refused as well, because the
     server reserves that prefix.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-path-shortest">
    <title>Shortest and Cheapest Paths</title>

    <para>
     Three functions take a pattern rather than a value, and each one stands where a whole
     pattern stands. All three take a chain of exactly one edge between two nodes. A longer
     chain raises <literal>only one relationship is allowed</literal>.
    </para>

    <para>
     <literal>shortestpath()</literal> returns one path of minimum length, or no row if there
     is none. It is a bidirectional search that stops as soon as the two frontiers meet, so it
     does not enumerate the paths it rejects. <literal>allshortestpaths()</literal> is the same
     search returning every path of that minimum length rather than one of them. Both accept a
     variable length edge, and the upper bound is what keeps an untyped search affordable.
    </para>

    <para>
     Both endpoints of a shortest path search have to be bound to variables, even when the
     names are never used. Leaving one out raises
     <literal>node must be a reference to a specific node</literal>. This is the one place
     where leaving a variable out is refused rather than merely wasteful.
    </para>

    <para>
     <literal>dijkstra()</literal> minimises total weight rather than edge count, and the two
     are often different paths. It takes a weight expression naming the edge variable bound
     inside its own pattern, evaluated once per edge as the search expands. Its pattern holds
     one plain edge, because the function does its own expansion: a <literal>*</literal> in
     there raises <literal>variable length relationship is not supported</literal>.
    </para>

    <para>
     A third argument is a qualifier, a boolean the search applies to each candidate edge, so
     edges can be excluded without filtering the graph first. <literal>LIMIT</literal> inside
     the parentheses caps how many paths come back, and its default is one.
     <literal>(p, total) = dijkstra(...)</literal> binds two things, the path and the total
     weight along it, while <literal>p = dijkstra(...)</literal> binds the path alone.
    </para>
   </refsect2>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1 id="cypher-expr">
   <title>Cypher Expressions</title>

   <para>
    A Cypher expression is built the way a SQL expression is, from literals, variables,
    operators and function calls. The difference is the data. A label's properties are one
    <type>jsonb</type> object per element, so <literal>n.name</literal> reads a member of that
    object and the result is <type>jsonb</type>, not a SQL scalar. Arithmetic, comparison and
    the string operators all run against <type>jsonb</type> values.
   </para>

   <para>
    An expression may appear wherever a clause takes one: a <literal>WHERE</literal> condition,
    a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> item, a property value in a pattern, a
    <xref linkend="cypher-set"/> right side, a sort key, a function argument.
   </para>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-expr-precedence">
    <title>Operator Precedence</title>

    <para>
     Operators bind in this order, loosest first. Two operators on the same row have equal
     precedence and associate as the row says.
    </para>

    <informaltable>
     <tgroup cols="3">
      <thead>
       <row>
        <entry>Precedence</entry>
        <entry>Operators</entry>
        <entry>Associativity</entry>
       </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
       <row><entry>1 (loosest)</entry><entry><literal>UNION</literal> <literal>EXCEPT</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>2</entry><entry><literal>INTERSECT</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>3</entry><entry><literal>OR</literal> <literal>XOR</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>4</entry><entry><literal>AND</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>5</entry><entry><literal>NOT</literal></entry><entry>right</entry></row>
       <row><entry>6</entry><entry><literal>IS NULL</literal> <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal> <literal>IS [NOT] UNKNOWN</literal></entry><entry>none</entry></row>
       <row><entry>7</entry><entry><literal>&lt;</literal> <literal>&lt;=</literal> <literal>&gt;</literal> <literal>&gt;=</literal> <literal>=</literal> <literal>&lt;&gt;</literal> <literal>!=</literal></entry><entry>none</entry></row>
       <row><entry>8</entry><entry><literal>IN</literal> <literal>BETWEEN</literal></entry><entry>none</entry></row>
       <row><entry>9</entry><entry>any other operator, including <literal>||</literal> and the <type>jsonb</type> operators</entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>10</entry><entry><literal>+</literal> <literal>-</literal> (binary)</entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>11</entry><entry><literal>*</literal> <literal>/</literal> <literal>%</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>12</entry><entry><literal>^</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>13</entry><entry><literal>-</literal> (unary)</entry><entry>right</entry></row>
       <row><entry>14</entry><entry><literal>STARTS WITH</literal> <literal>ENDS WITH</literal> <literal>CONTAINS</literal> <literal>=~</literal></entry><entry>none</entry></row>
       <row><entry>15</entry><entry><literal>[ ]</literal> (subscript), <literal>( )</literal> (call)</entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>16</entry><entry><literal>::</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
       <row><entry>17 (tightest)</entry><entry><literal>.</literal></entry><entry>left</entry></row>
      </tbody>
     </tgroup>
    </informaltable>

    <para>
     Three rows differ from a common expectation. <literal>^</literal> is left associative and
     binds looser than unary minus, which is PostgreSQL's rule rather than arithmetic's, so
     <literal>2 ^ 3 ^ 2</literal> is <literal>(2 ^ 3) ^ 2</literal> and
     <literal>-2 ^ 2</literal> is <literal>(-2) ^ 2</literal>. The string matching operators
     bind tighter than concatenation and tighter than arithmetic. And comparisons do not chain:
     <literal>1 &lt; 2 &lt; 3</literal> is a syntax error rather than two comparisons.
    </para>

    <para>
     SQL has a few operators at levels of its own, <literal>AT TIME ZONE</literal>,
     <literal>COLLATE</literal>, <literal>LIKE</literal>, <literal>ILIKE</literal> and
     <literal>SIMILAR TO</literal>. Cypher's expression grammar accepts none of them.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-expr-strings">
    <title>String Matching Operators</title>

    <informaltable>
     <tgroup cols="2">
      <thead>
       <row>
        <entry>Operator</entry>
        <entry>Test</entry>
       </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
       <row><entry><replaceable>string</replaceable> <literal>STARTS WITH</literal> <replaceable>prefix</replaceable></entry><entry>The string begins with the prefix.</entry></row>
       <row><entry><replaceable>string</replaceable> <literal>ENDS WITH</literal> <replaceable>suffix</replaceable></entry><entry>The string ends with the suffix.</entry></row>
       <row><entry><replaceable>string</replaceable> <literal>CONTAINS</literal> <replaceable>substring</replaceable></entry><entry>The substring occurs anywhere in the string.</entry></row>
       <row><entry><replaceable>string</replaceable> <literal>=~</literal> <replaceable>pattern</replaceable></entry><entry>The whole string matches the POSIX regular expression.</entry></row>
      </tbody>
     </tgroup>
    </informaltable>

    <para>
     PostgreSQL's regular expression operators <literal>~</literal>, <literal>~*</literal>,
     <literal>!~</literal> and <literal>!~*</literal> also work in Cypher. None of the four
     operators above can be answered from an index. A predicate that has to be sargable needs
     <literal>=</literal> or a range.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-expr-comments">
    <title>Comments</title>

    <para>
     A comment runs from <literal>--</literal> to the end of the line, or from
     <literal>/*</literal> to a matching <literal>*/</literal>. Block comments nest, so a
     commented out region containing a comment stays balanced. <literal>//</literal> is not a
     comment and is a syntax error.
    </para>
   </refsect2>

   <refsect2 id="cypher-expr-identifiers">
    <title>Identifiers and Case Folding</title>

    <para>
     An identifier names a graph, a label, a variable, a property key, a column, an index or a
     constraint. An unquoted identifier folds to lower case, exactly as PostgreSQL folds an
     unquoted SQL identifier, so <literal>MOVIE</literal>, <literal>Movie</literal> and
     <literal>movie</literal> all name the same label. A double quoted identifier is taken as
     written, and <literal>"MOVIE"</literal> is a different name from <literal>movie</literal>.
     A quoted variable has to be quoted at every mention.
    </para>

    <para>
     Property keys fold the same way. <literal>{MixedKey: 1}</literal> stores the key
     <literal>mixedkey</literal> and <literal>{"MixedKey": 1}</literal> stores
     <literal>MixedKey</literal>. The two are different keys and one element can carry both, so
     quote a key consistently or not at all.
    </para>

    <para>
     An unquoted identifier starts with a letter or an underscore and continues with letters,
     digits, underscores and dollar signs. Letter includes non ASCII letters. The length limit
     is PostgreSQL's 63 bytes, counted in bytes rather than characters, and a longer name is
     truncated. Backticks are not a quoting character: <literal>`movie`</literal> is a syntax
     error. A string literal takes single quotes, so double quotes never make a string.
    </para>

    <para>
     A reserved word is read as a keyword and not as a name, so a pattern variable cannot be
     called <literal>both</literal>, <literal>let</literal>, <literal>finish</literal>,
     <literal>insert</literal>, <literal>offset</literal> or <literal>xor</literal>. Quoting
     lifts the restriction, because a quoted identifier is never read as a keyword. A reserved
     word is still allowed as a <command>RETURN</command> alias and as a property key, since
     neither position can be confused with a keyword.
    </para>
   </refsect2>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1 id="cypher-path-examples">
   <title>Examples</title>

   <para>
    These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
   </para>

   <para>
    A range unions the lengths in it, so the shorter reaches come back alongside the longer
    ones:
<programlisting>
MATCH (a:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})-[:related_to*1..2]-&gt;(b:movie)
RETURN b.title AS reached ORDER BY reached;

           reached
------------------------------
 "Inception"
 "Terminator 2: Judgment Day"
 "The Matrix Reloaded"
 "The Matrix Revolutions"
 "The Terminator"
 "Total Recall"
(6 rows)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    A named path can be taken apart. The <literal>[x IN list | expression]</literal> form is a
    list comprehension, and it is how a route is rendered readably:
<programlisting>
MATCH p = (a:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})-[:related_to*2]-&gt;(b:movie)
RETURN length(p) AS hops,
       [x IN nodes(p) | x.title] AS route,
       [r IN relationships(p) | r.weight] AS weights
ORDER BY route;

 hops |                              route                              | weights
------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------
 2    | ["The Matrix", "The Matrix Reloaded", "The Matrix Revolutions"] | [1, 1]
 2    | ["The Matrix", "The Terminator", "Terminator 2: Judgment Day"]  | [3, 1]
 2    | ["The Matrix", "Total Recall", "Inception"]                     | [4, 2]
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    <literal>shortestpath()</literal> over an untyped, undirected edge crosses two edge labels,
    forwards along one and backwards along the other:
<programlisting>
MATCH p = shortestpath((a:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[e*..6]-(b:person {name: 'Arnold Schwarzenegger'}))
RETURN length(p) AS hops,
       [x IN nodes(p) | label(x)] AS labels,
       [r IN relationships(p) | label(r)] AS via;

 hops |                 labels                 |                  via
------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------
 3    | ["person", "movie", "movie", "person"] | ["acted_in", "related_to", "acted_in"]
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    Both ends have to be named, even when the names are never read:
<programlisting>
MATCH p = shortestpath((:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[e*..6]-(:person {name: 'Arnold Schwarzenegger'}))
RETURN length(p);

ERROR:  node must be a reference to a specific node
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    <literal>allshortestpaths()</literal> returns one row per route of the minimum length.
    There is one here, because this dataset's <literal>related_to</literal> edges form a tree:
<programlisting>
MATCH p = allshortestpaths((a:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})-[:related_to*..4]-&gt;(b:movie {title: 'Inception'}))
RETURN length(p) AS hops, [x IN nodes(p) | x.title] AS route;

 hops |                    route
------+---------------------------------------------
 2    | ["The Matrix", "Total Recall", "Inception"]
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    <literal>dijkstra()</literal> minimises the weight. The two variable form binds the total
    as well as the path:
<programlisting>
MATCH (a:movie {title: 'The Matrix'}), (b:movie {title: 'Inception'})
MATCH (p, total) = dijkstra((a)-[e:related_to]-&gt;(b), e.weight)
RETURN total AS weight, [x IN nodes(p) | x.title] AS route, [r IN relationships(p) | r.weight] AS legs;

 weight |                    route                    |  legs
--------+---------------------------------------------+--------
 6      | ["The Matrix", "Total Recall", "Inception"] | [4, 2]
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    An unquoted label name folds to lower case, so <literal>MOVIE</literal> finds the
    <literal>movie</literal> label:
<programlisting>
MATCH (n:MOVIE {title: 'The Matrix'}) RETURN n.title AS title;

    title
--------------
 "The Matrix"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    Quoting keeps the case as written. No label is called <literal>MOVIE</literal>, so
    <literal>(n:"MOVIE")</literal> matches nothing rather than failing.
   </para>

   <para>
    <literal>^</literal> groups to the left and binds looser than unary minus:
<programlisting>
RETURN 2 ^ 3 ^ 2 AS pow, -2 ^ 2 AS neg;

 pow | neg
-----+-----
 64  | 4
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    The four string matching operators:
<programlisting>
RETURN 'Matrix' STARTS WITH 'Mat' AS sw, 'Matrix' ENDS WITH 'rix' AS ew,
       'Matrix' CONTAINS 'atr' AS c, 'Matrix' =~ '^M.*x$' AS re;

 sw | ew | c | re
----+----+---+----
 t  | t  | t | t
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    Both comment forms, in one statement:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})   -- one film
/* a block comment
   over two lines */
RETURN m.title AS title;

    title
--------------
 "The Matrix"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1 id="cypher-path-notes">
   <title>Notes</title>

   <para>
    Within one pattern match no edge is used twice, so a two hop pattern never doubles back
    along the edge it arrived on. A node variable may be repeated, which constrains both
    positions to the same vertex, but an edge variable may be used once per pattern and
    reusing it raises <literal>duplicate variable</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    A pattern is not a value. It cannot be used as a boolean, so
    <literal>WHERE (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(:movie)</literal> does not parse. Wrap it in
    <literal>EXISTS { MATCH ... }</literal>, described in
    <xref linkend="cypher-subqueryexpressions"/>. A label test is
    <literal>label(n) = 'movie'</literal>, not a colon in a condition.
   </para>

   <para>
    A variable length edge appears in a plan as
    <literal>Graph VLE on <replaceable>label</replaceable> [min..max]</literal>. Left
    unlabelled it becomes <literal>Graph VLE on ag_edge</literal>, which walks every edge label
    in the graph at every hop, so naming the label is the single largest thing that reduces the
    cost of a traversal.
   </para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1 id="cypher-path-compatibility">
   <title>Compatibility</title>

   <para>
    The standard's label expression, ISO/IEC 39075:2024 section 16.8, combines label names with
    <literal>|</literal>, <literal>&amp;</literal> and <literal>!</literal>. AgensGraph accepts
    a single label name per element, so none of the three is available. The standard's element
    pattern filler, section 16.7, has no <literal>ONLY</literal>; AgensGraph adds it because a
    label here is a table that child labels inherit from. The graph pattern quantifier of
    section 16.11 is written <literal>{1,3}</literal> after an element; AgensGraph writes the
    same idea as <literal>*1..3</literal> inside the edge brackets.
    <literal>shortestpath</literal>, <literal>allshortestpaths</literal> and
    <literal>dijkstra</literal> are not in the standard.
   </para>

   <para>
    Three lexical differences will catch a reader who knows Cypher from elsewhere. Identifiers
    fold to lower case and backticks are not a quoting character. A string literal takes single
    quotes only. And <literal>//</literal> is not a comment. <literal>XOR</literal>,
    <literal>STARTS WITH</literal>, <literal>ENDS WITH</literal>, <literal>CONTAINS</literal>
    and <literal>=~</literal> are all present and mean what they mean in openCypher.
   </para>
  </refsect1>

